{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to brilliant.org for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2820,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life as we know it is carbon-based, \nbut does it have to be that way?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7440,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is another element on the \nperiodic table that shares some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10200,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the key properties of carbon but \nis far more abundant on most planets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14040,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’m talking about silicon. So could \nthere silicon-based life out there?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 24840,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All of the chemical machinery of life - \nall biochemistry - is based around the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29280,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carboan atom. At least as far as we know. If \nwe want to understand the possible diversity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34200,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and abundance of life in the universe, we \nneed to ask whether this is the only option"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39360,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most popular alternative - at least in \nscience fiction - is silicon-based life. We have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 45720,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the xenomorphs of the Alien series to the giant \nspace worm in Star Wars and many, many others.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52260,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So why are scifi writers so into silicon-based \nlife? Because it really is the most promising  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58620,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-carbon prospect due to its chemical \nsimilarity. It’s also more abundant on Earth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64320,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than carbon. So, can life be based on silicon? \nAnd if so, why aren’t we silicon-based?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70980,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we say life is carbon-based, we don’t \njust mean that life uses carbon a lot - we  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75840,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mean that literally every aspect of the \nmolecular machinery that makes up life is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80280,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "based around the chemistry of carbon. Carbon \nchains and rings form the scaffolding for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86040,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an enormous variety of molecules which \nenable the raw machinery of life - from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 90300,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "DNA \u0026 RNA to amino acids \u0026 proteins \nto all the structures built by these."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94740,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Biochemistry is by definition \nthe chemistry of Carbon. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98640,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "biochemists don't even write Carbon in their \nformulas, they just draw bent lines and they  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103680,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "know that's supposed to be a Carbon atom. So why \nis carbon so favored by life? Is it just that the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110100,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first life form happened to be carbon based, \nso everything else got stuck with that atom?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114420,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or is carbon-based chemistry really the \nonly way life can happen anywhere, ever?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119760,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ultimately, carbon’s incredible utility comes \ndown to the way it bonds with other elements,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125460,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which enable just the right \nbalance of reactivity and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128520,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stability. To understand this, \nwe need to think about energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132240,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nature will always try to get to the lowest energy \nstate that it possibly can. A stick of dynamite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138420,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example, is in a high energy state due to \nall the energy stored in the chemical bonds of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144360,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its nitroglycerine. If the atoms find a way \nto a lower energy state they’ll go for it,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150900,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explosively releasing that energy in the \nprocess of converting the nitro to smaller  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155760,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules. Nitroglycerine is unstable \nbecause it only takes a little energy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160800,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to break its tenuous bonds, which in turn \nreleases a lot more energy than went in,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165180,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "initiating a chain reaction. That reaction \nonly goes in one direction, because it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170520,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not energetically favourable to reassemble \nall the smaller molecules back into nitro."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176640,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Life is basically an ongoing chemical \nreaction. You don’t want the chemistry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182700,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of life to be unidirectional. You don’t \nwant all the raw ingredients to end up in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same overly-stable molecules, with no \nway back. That means the reaction is over,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192840,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which for life means …well, death. At the \nsame time, you want the molecules doing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199140,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the work of life to be stable enough - they \nneed to last long enough to do their jobs."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204420,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, you want a balance \nof reactivity and stability. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209760,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s figure out what elements might \nbe good for reaching this balance.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214140,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to start we’re going to open up to the entire \nperiodic table and see what we can rule out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 219540,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The key is in what types of chemical bonds a given \nelement can make. There are three main bond types:  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225360,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ionic, metallic, and covalent. \nIonic bonds are way too unstable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230220,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while metallic bonds result in repeating \nstructures - crystals - which don’t have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234720,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the chemical variety needed for life. \nCovalent bonds are really the only option."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240540,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In these, atoms share electrons with \none or more neighbors in order to fill  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245100,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up their outer or valence shells. For example, \ncarbon has 4 valence electrons. It wants 8 for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252120,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maximum stability. It achieves that stability \nby connecting to 4 hydrogen atoms and sharing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258840,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "their single electron. This also gives each \nof those hydrogen atoms one more electron  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263340,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to fill up its own valence shells. The \nresult of this arrangement is methane."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269220,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The cool thing about covalent bonds is that there \nare countless ways to do this electron sharing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273660,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enabling enormous diversity \nand modularity. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277800,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we remove a hydrogen from two methane \nmolecules and stick them together you have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ethane. Now replace one of the hydrogens \nwith an oxygen and we have ethanol.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 289020,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Covalent bonds are the key to the complex and \nresilient molecular structures needed for life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295020,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this in mind we can look at the Periodic \nTable and rule out all the elements that form  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299520,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mostly ionic or metallic bonds, which is actually \nmost elements. We’re keeping things that are good  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305940,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at forming covalent bonds, even if they \nalso sometimes bond in those other ways."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310320,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can also rule out Noble Gasses since they are \nbarely reactive at all, and the Halogens, because  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315660,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’re just too much trouble. It's hard to get \nthem to form covalent bonds with more than one  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320100,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "element at the same time, and when they react with \nsomething it's usually in an explosive manner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325320,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally, we’re going to scrap elements in rows 4 \nand below. Although these may form covalent bonds,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331260,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these atoms are so large that the \nnucleus doesn't hold the electrons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335700,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with enough strength, leading \nto dangerously weak molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way, this is the reason Arsenic is toxic. \nCells can accidentally build molecules using  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345480,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Arsenic in place of Phosphorus due to them having \nthe same number of valence electrons. When that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 350580,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "happens the resulting molecules will break, \ndestroying important structures in the cell,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354600,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or interrupting important processes, and \nif this happens too much the cell will die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359400,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the end we are left with only seven elements: \nHydrogen, Carbon, Nitrogen, Oxygen, Silicon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365700,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Phosphorus, and Sulfur. Of these, all but silicon \nare considered fundamental building blocks of life  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371340,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Earth. Other elements are used by life for \nvery specific functions, but hydrogen, carbon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377700,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nitrogen, oxygen, phosphorus, and sulfur are \nthe most important. And of these, carbon is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382740,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by far the more critical because it provides the \nscaffolding of all biochemistry. The reason it’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388560,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "good for this is that it forms strong bonds with \nitself, and does so in a variety of ways. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395160,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the 6 building blocks of life, only carbon \ncan form both 1-D and 2-D structures with itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 401040,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our process of identifying elements that \nmay be useful for life we got to the 6 main  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406620,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "building blocks plus silicon. But why \ndid silicon get dropped by evolution?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411900,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems weird because silicon \nis chemically similar to carbon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416040,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "also having 4 valence electrons. Silicon \ncan imitate Carbon and form long chains,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421260,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and rings, and the resulting molecules often have \nsimilar properties to molecules made with Carbon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427740,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To give you just one example we can make \nSilicon oils, and they feel and look just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433440,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "like other kinds of oil. It’s the only \nother element capable of forming complex  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438480,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "covalent scaffolds and supporting a large \ndiversity of molecules. Despite all of this,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444360,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth life makes less use of silicon than it \ndoes of many other trace elements like iron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449700,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This seems extra strange when you remember \nthat silicon accounts for more than 28% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454560,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mass of Earth’s crust, in all them silicate \nrocks. On the other hand, Carbon makes up only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458160,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 0.02% of the crust. This tells us that \ncarbon must have substantial advantages over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465600,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicon. So let’s talk about some of the \nissues with silicon as a basis for life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471120,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One huge one is that silicon-based molecules \ntend to be far more reactive to water than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476340,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon-based. In fact, although \nsilicon chemistry is extremely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480420,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diverse, most complex silicon \nmolecules are unstable in water.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485220,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can see how this would be a problem for \nsilicon-based life on our very watery Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490860,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In general, life needs a solvent - a \nliquid medium that allows molecular  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495420,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "machinery to move around and interact. Water \nis incredible for this. Its thermal stability,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 501720,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "power as a solvent, and abundance \nthrough the universe make it the best,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505920,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and perhaps even the only decent choice of the \nbase solvent for life. The instability of most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512460,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicon molecules in water is actually a huge \nblow to the viability of silicon-based life."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517860,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However water is not the only option. A much \ngreater variety of silicon-based molecules  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524280,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are stable in liquid hydrocarbons - which \nare present on the moons of the gas giants,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529200,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and even in sulphuric acid, which \nis abundant in Venus’s atmosphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534060,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Both cases have their issues - liquid hydrocarbons \nare cold, which among other things means they have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540240,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "trouble holding in solution the large, complex \nmolecules that known life relies on. Sulphuric  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547620,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "acid is chemically extremely aggressive, \nperhaps too much so for life to get started."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553080,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, xenomorphs are silicon-based \nand they do have acidic blood - makes sense that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’re silicon -in-sulphuric-acid-based. \nIf that’s what Venusian life is like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564840,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s cancel the cloud city construction \nprogram before the space horror begins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 570060,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is one way to potentially get silicon-based \nlife in water - and that’s by coupling silicon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575100,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with either carbon or oxygen for our scaffolding \nmolecule. Chains of these pairs of atoms can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580800,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce some chemical diversity. However neither \ncase seems to provide significant advantage over  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586860,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just using carbon chains. Another reason that \nsilicon seems implausible as a scaffolding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592380,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "element for life is that the stuff really, \nreally loves forming bonds with oxygen.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597960,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Silicon forms stronger bonds with oxygen than \nwith any of the other “building blocks of life”,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602580,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and certainly more so than \nwith other silicon atoms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605580,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Silicon-based molecules are \neasily broken by oxygen,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609660,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and silicon-oxygen bonds are hard to \nbreak in turn. That means reactions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 614700,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tend to go in one direction when there’s \noxygen available. And the strength of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619320,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicon-oxygen bond is perhaps the main reason \nthat silicon is rarely used by life on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624360,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although there’s an awful lot of \nit, Earth’s silicon is locked up in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628620,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rocks and is inaccessible to life. On the \nother hand, most of Earth’s carbon is in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634320,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "CO2 in the atmosphere, which is relatively \neasily accessible through photosynthesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is one sense in which the strength of the \nsilicon-oxygen bond could be useful for life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644700,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that’s in energy generation. Let’s \nthink about where our energy comes from.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650220,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s from the conversion of oxygen molecules \n- which contain a ton of energy in their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654540,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bonds - into CO2 molecules - which contain very \nlittle. The simplest way to get that energy is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660240,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to set something on fire, which works great \nfor steam engines but not so great for cells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665040,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The mitochondria in our eukariot cells \nliberate that energy with a few more steps,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671100,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "finally storing it in the ATP molecule. So \ncould silicon-based aliens get energy by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676620,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "converting O2 into silicon-dioxide? \nOn the surface this looks good,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681720,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because SIO2 is even more stable than \nCO2, so there’s more energy to liberate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688380,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The problem is that while CO2 is \na gas that can be expelled easily,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693240,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "silicon-dioxide is silica … it’s sand. It’s \ncoarse and rough and gets everywhere, I hate it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701520,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not something you want building up in your \ncells or your lungs. And this wouldn't be a small  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708540,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of sand. A regular person exhales about \na kilogram of Carbon Dioxide throughout the day,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713340,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meaning Silicon aliens would need to cough out \na good amount of sand. Or they poop glass beads  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720240,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or something - probably there’s a way around the \nissue. But it’s certainly not as convenient and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724740,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy-cheap as exhaling CO2. And with life, \nany little saving in energy is a big deal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731280,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s summarize. In certain conditions silicon \npotentially has the chemical diversity for an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737640,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien biochemistry, but those conditions are \npretty specialized, and are far less ideal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742980,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the ones that support carbon-based life. It \nseems that if life has the option of using carbon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749040,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then it should do so. But it’s not impossible that \nin highly unusual environments silicon-based life"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756840,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what would that life even look like?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760200,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For decades science fiction authors have \nimagined silicon based life as variations  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764820,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of living crystals or rocks, that's because that’s \nthe common form of silicon on Earth is just that.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 771180,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we carbon-based creatures don’t look \nlike walking diamonds or pencil leads,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775620,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so there’s no reason to think of silicon-based \naliens would look particularly crystally."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781740,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, actually. There is a reason. And that’s \nbecause living silicon crystals already exist  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787200,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on Earth. Let me present you to Diatoms. These \nare single celled organism that have incorporated  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 794100,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Silicon into their cell walls, making them \nrigid, and basically just Silica crystals  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799320,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that encapsulate all the parts of the cell inside \nthem. These beautiful living crystals live simple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805620,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lives. They just float around in the ocean \nin long chains photosynthesizing, producing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811980,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "half of the oxygen that we breath. Now to be \nclear, Diatoms are not Silicon based life,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817380,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it's just their cell walls. Everything inside \nthem is based on Carbon. But they are at least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823800,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a proof of concept that one important component of \nlife - the cell wall - can be built from silicon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what have we learned? Well, silicon-based \naliens are probably slightly over-represented in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836400,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fiction compared to their likely frequency \nout there in the universe. In fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841620,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "carbon-in-water is such an astoundingly good \noption for biochemistry that there may be no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847440,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other game in town. Even Carl Sagan called \nhimself a carbon-chauvinist for this reason.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 853140,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll probably be more similar than different to \nthe alien species of our first-contact. But let’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 859680,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not ignore the hydrocarbon lakes of Titan or the \nacid cloud-scapes of Venus, where silicon-based  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866880,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "critters may expand our notion of what is \npossible in the variety of life across space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873960,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant.org for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant is an online learning platform for STEM \nwith hands-on, interactive lessons. Brilliant is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882600,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for curious learners, both young and old, \nprofessional and inexperienced. Brilliant  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886920,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "courses teach you how to think via interactive \nlessons and problem-solving activities/exercises.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893040,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, Brilliant.org offers \na course called Quantum Objects.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897120,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The principles of quantum mechanics came \nfrom investigating microscopic phenomena,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902040,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bizarre behavior of quantum objects like atoms \nand elementary particles that often appear to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 907260,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "contradict classical mechanics and probability. \nIn this course, you'll explore experiments of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913860,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum objects and use them to construct new \nequations of motion, new laws of physics, and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919740,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new system of measurement based not on numbers \nbut on algebras. By the end, you’ll gain a new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 926340,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "appreciation for how the physics of the small \nenables lasers, transistors, and other modern  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933000,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technologies that define our world. Then you'll \nbe ready to dive into the ongoing revolution of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum information and computing. To learn more \nabout Brilliant, go to brilliant.org/spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re doing comment responses for the \nlast two episodes - the one about the new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 949680,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simulations that show how our moon formed from \na giant impact with higher resolution than ever  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955860,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before. And the one on Max Tegmark’s mathematical \nuniverse hypothesis which proposes that the most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962280,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fundamental reality is a platonic realm of \nmathematics. We’ll start with the latter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967620,
    "dDurationMs": 8100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "onebronx opines that the basic math of our \nUniverse is likely not the SM lagrangian  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975720,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the GR equation, but the principle of \na stationary action and a specific set of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 983280,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mutually-consistent symmetries. I absolutely \nagree on the core point - which is that IF  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991020,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mathematical universe hypothesis is true, \na given universe probably shouldn’t defined  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996060,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by the platonic existence of a complicated \nset of equations, but rather a very simple  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001220,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "set of rules. In the mathematical universe \nhypothesis, the physical world is emergent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006440,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a more fundamental mathematical world. \nBut that doesn’t mean that complex mathematical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1011960,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structures can’t also be emergent from simpler, \nmore fundamental mathematical structures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017420,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "S. Lee Odegard mentions that we should have \nincluded Sir Rodger Penrose's three worlds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023360,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ontology, in which reality is described as three \nrealms of existence, each merging from the other.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I agree, and this was totally going to be in the \nepisode but we ran out of space for it. To give an  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035600,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overview - Penrose proposes this way of thinking \nabout the circular emergence of reality. We have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042680,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the material world as emerging from a platonic \nmathematical world - in the sense that without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047720,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the possibility of mathematical regularities, \nthe material world couldn’t exist; and then  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1054140,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mental world emerges from the material - in \nthe sense that material brains generate mind;  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and finally the platonic world emerges from \nthe mental - in the sense that minds are able  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067520,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to conceive of the full language of mathematics \neven beyond its manifestation in the physical.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1073460,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is pretty different from Tegmark’s idea \nthat the platonic math world is the ontological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "precursor to everything else. Penrose doesn’t \ngrant primacy to the platonic - which is why  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085520,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I ultimately decided to leave that \nout - perhaps for a future episode.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1089780,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If anyone wants to read about this, get Penrose’s \nbook Road to Reality - but fair warning,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1095540,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reading it is a full semester intensive physics \ncourse. Which is, by the way, a recommendation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1101780,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, and now on to the far less abstract \nidea that the moon formed when a planet  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1106880,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we name Theia smashed \ninto the newly formed Earth"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109820,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "markcentral mentioned an idea I’d never \nheard before. That lumps of relatively  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115460,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "intact Theia material may still \nbe present in the earth’s mantle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1120380,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are these high density blobs of material \nthat sit at the boundary between Earth’s mantle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126860,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and core called the Large low-shear-velocity \nprovinces. Some scientists have suggested that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133220,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this is material from the planet that collided \nwith Earth in the Moon’s formation. There is some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138860,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "loose evidence that isotopic ratios in basalt \nthought to be from lava plumes rising from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146060,
    "dDurationMs": 7500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions above the LLSVPs have “primitive” isotopic \nratios consistent with material that wasn’t mixed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153560,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the giant impact. Very cool possibility, \nthough obviously a lot more work is needed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1158780,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alice asks: If the moon is a very unlikely event, \nand an unusually strong iron core is necessary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1166460,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to protect the development of life, does this \nprovide a sufficiently hard filter to resolve  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171680,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Fermi paradox? The answer is: we don’t know, \nbut people have speculated that this might be the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1177500,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "case. My personal intuition is that the extremely \nhard filter needed to explain the Fermi paradox  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1183560,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is actually a chain of somewhat hard filters, \nand the large moon may be one of these. Others  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1191240,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "include having a planetary composition like ours \n- large protective gas giants in the outer solar  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197720,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "system but only small terrestrial planets in \nclose, a star of a very particular quality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1203420,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so on. Check our video on the rare \nearth hypothesis for more on this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1207740,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’d like to address a comment about my \ncomment response from the supercritical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1213260,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fluids episode where I joked that there are \nanimals with hands large enough to swim in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1218480,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gas - they’re called birds. Several people noted \nthat bats would be a better example here because,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1226340,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as Xovvo notes, and I quote “Bat wings are formed \nfrom membranous extensions from palm to fingertip  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1234380,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between digits two through five. Bird hands \nare actually very small and have completely  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1240140,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fused together into a structure called the \ncarpometaparpus, tipped by greatly reduced  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1246740,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "digits II and III (digit I bearing the alula, \nanterior to the rest of the carpometacarpus).  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1253460,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Birds just have very long feathers to form \ntheir flight surface. So … I’ve learned a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1261020,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lot from my mistake - both regarding the size \nof flighted animal hands, and that even casual  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1266360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jokes should be subject to rigorous research and \nfact-checking. My sincere apologies to everyone"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1271760,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Several people noticed that I flubbed when \nnaming Neptune’s moon Triton. I misspoke  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1277340,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and said Titan, which is of course Saturns moon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1280280,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks to those in the comments who wondered if \nit might just be my Australian accent - like,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1285200,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we don’t pronounce r’s after t’s or something. \nYeah, that’s it - just remember any time you hear  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1291020,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "me misspeak it’s actually just how Aussies \npronounce the actual correct word. Also,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1295580,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all Australian birds have gigantic hands, \nunlike everywhere else in the world."
    } ]
  } ]
}
